===============================================================================
 L.O.C.K.I. - Live Optical Custody & Key Identifier
 STEP-BY-STEP SETUP & USER GUIDE
===============================================================================

Real-time computer-vision system that tracks key checkouts at shipping
centers: identifies the driver by face, watches the key board by color,
detects hand grabs, logs every custody event to SQLite, and saves MP4
evidence clips.

Requirements: Python 3.10+ (tested on 3.12), a webcam, and optionally a
second display for the wall signage.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 - CREATE A VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

From the project root:

    python3 -m venv .venv
    source .venv/bin/activate        # Linux / macOS
    # .venv\Scripts\activate         # Windows (PowerShell: .venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1)

You should see (.venv) at the start of your prompt. Activate it every time
you open a new terminal before running any command below.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 - INSTALL PYTHON PACKAGES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Core (required):

    pip install -r requirements.txt          # numpy, opencv, pygame

Optional extras:

    pip install mediapipe                    # hand-grasp detection
    pip install insightface onnxruntime      # face identification

    NOTE: insightface downloads its model pack (~30 MB) on first run into
    ~/.insightface. mediapipe downloads its model (~7 MB) into models/.

Linux servers / Docker also need the GL system libraries:

    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install -y libegl1 libgl1 libgles2

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 - QUICK TEST (NO CAMERA NEEDED)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run the built-in scripted demo:

    python main.py --demo --no-pygame

Over ~15 seconds it simulates three custody outcomes on a synthetic key
board:

    SUCCESS              - Driver_101 takes their assigned red key
    WRONG_ROUTE_ALERT    - Driver_102 (blue) takes a red key
    UNAUTHORIZED_REMOVAL - an unidentified person takes a green key

Each event writes an MP4 evidence clip into evidence/ and a row into the
SQLite database. Verify with:

    sqlite3 data/logs/key_events.db "SELECT id, driver_id, taken_route, status, clip_path FROM checkouts;"

Press q or ESC to quit early. If you see all three events, the
installation works.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 - ENROLL DRIVER FACES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Create one folder per driver inside data/faces/ named exactly after the
driver_id used in the schedule, and drop 3-5 clear, well-lit face photos
(JPG/PNG) into each:

    data/faces/Driver_101/photo1.jpg
    data/faces/Driver_101/photo2.jpg
    data/faces/Driver_102/photo1.jpg
    ...

Tips: faces should be large (>60 px), front-facing, and without sunglasses.
Multiple photos per driver improve matching. New photos need an engine
restart (or call FaceRecognizer.rebuild_gallery()).

If you skip this step the system still runs - every person is treated as
UNIDENTIFIED and key removals are logged as UNAUTHORIZED_REMOVAL.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 - SET THE DAILY SCHEDULE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Edit config/schedule.csv. Columns (header row required):

    driver_id,assigned_route
    Driver_101,Route_RED
    Driver_102,Route_BLUE
    Driver_103,Route_GREEN
    Driver_104,Route_ORANGE

Route text is case/spacing tolerant ("route blue" works too) but must
contain one of the color words red / blue / green / orange, otherwise the
engine refuses to start (bad rows point you at the exact line).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 6 - CHECK THE CAMERA CONFIGURATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Open config/config.json and review:

    "camera": {
        "device_index": 0,        <- 0 = first webcam, 1 = second, ...
        "frame_width": 1920,      <- must match what the camera DELIVERS
        "frame_height": 1080,
        "fps": 30,
        "buffer_seconds": 5       <- evidence buffer length
    }

IMPORTANT: the slot ROIs ("slots" section, [x1, y1, x2, y2] pixel boxes)
must line up with the physical key positions in the camera's view at the
configured resolution. If the camera delivers a different resolution than
requested, a warning is logged at startup and ROIs will be misaligned -
fix frame_width/height first.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 7 - CALIBRATE THE COLORS (IMPORTANT)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Room lighting changes through the day; HSV thresholds must match YOUR
lighting. Point the camera at the key board, then run:

    python calibrate_hsv.py

Controls:
    - H/S/V Min and H/S/V Max trackbars : tune the color window
    - h          : cycle red -> blue -> green -> orange
    - s          : save current values to config/config.json
    - p          : print values to the console
    - r          : reset to last saved values
    - q / ESC    : quit

For each color: hold that fob in view and adjust until the right half of
the preview (the mask) shows the fob in white and nothing else. Saving
creates a timestamped backup of the config automatically.

Other useful flags:
    python calibrate_hsv.py --camera 1          # second camera
    python calibrate_hsv.py --width 1280 --height 720

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 8 - RUN THE SYSTEM
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    python main.py

That starts everything: video window, wall signage (pygame), audio cues,
hand tracking, face recognition, database logging.

Useful variants:

    python main.py --camera 1        # use a specific camera
    python main.py --no-pygame       # OpenCV overlay only (no wall monitor)
    python main.py --no-audio        # silent operation
    python main.py --max-frames 100  # exit after N frames (testing)
    python main.py --demo            # scripted synthetic board
    python main.py -v                # debug logging

Exit with q or ESC in the video window, or Ctrl+C in the terminal.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 9 - WHAT YOU SEE IN OPERATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Video window (main overlay):
    - Green box   : key present and route correct
    - Gray box    : slot empty / inactive
    - Red box     : wrong-route key in the slot
    - Amber box   : unauthorized-removal alert
    - Top banner  : identified driver + their route, event messages

Wall signage window (separate, if pygame enabled):
    - Green border + chime  : valid checkout in progress / completed
    - Flashing red + alarm  : WRONG ROUTE ALERT
    - Flashing amber + alarm: UNAUTHORIZED REMOVAL
    - Blue border           : idle / monitoring

For every checkout the system:
    1. Saves a 3-second MP4 pre-incident clip into evidence/
    2. Writes a row into the checkouts table with the clip path
    3. Statuses: SUCCESS | WRONG_ROUTE_ALERT | UNAUTHORIZED_REMOVAL

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 10 - REVIEWING THE LOGS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Database (SQLite at data/logs/key_events.db):

    sqlite3 data/logs/key_events.db

    -- last 20 events
    SELECT * FROM checkouts ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20;

    -- only alerts
    SELECT * FROM checkouts WHERE status != 'SUCCESS';

    -- one driver's history
    SELECT * FROM checkouts WHERE driver_id = 'Driver_101';

    -- events that have evidence clips
    SELECT driver_id, status, clip_path FROM checkouts WHERE clip_path != '';

JSON export from Python:

    from src.database import DatabaseManager
    db = DatabaseManager()
    db.export_json("events.json", limit=500)
    db.close()

Evidence clips play in any video player (VLC, mpv).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TROUBLESHOOTING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

"CameraStreamError: Failed to open camera N after 5 attempts"
    - Wrong device_index, or the camera is used by another program.
    - Try: python main.py --camera 1
    - Linux permission check: ls -l /dev/video0 (add yourself to the
      "video" group if needed).

"Hand tracking unavailable ... grasp events disabled"
    - mediapipe is not installed or its model failed to load. Install it,
      or keep running without grasp triggers.

"Face recognition unavailable"
    - insightface/onnxruntime missing. Install them; the system keeps
      running with UNIDENTIFIED as the driver id (conservative mode).

"UNLISTED DRIVER" banner
    - The face matched an enrolled folder that is not in schedule.csv.
      Add the driver to the schedule or fix the folder name.

Slot boxes do not line up with the keys
    - Camera delivered a different resolution than config.json requests.
      Check the startup warning, fix frame_width/height, or re-map the
      slot ROIs.

No audio / SDL audio errors
    - Run with --no-audio, or set "audio_backend" in the "ui" section of
      config.json (e.g. "pulse", "alsa", "dummy").

Evidence clip is shorter than 3 s on the first event
    - Normal: the rolling buffer needs ~5 s of runtime to fill up.

Python version errors installing mediapipe/insightface
    - These pin specific Python versions. Create a separate venv with the
      Python they support, or run without them (system degrades
      gracefully).

Headless server (no monitor)
    - Use --no-pygame; the video window is skipped automatically when no
      display is detected. Detection, logging, and evidence clips all
      still work.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROJECT LAYOUT (REFERENCE)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    main.py                     <- run this
    calibrate_hsv.py            <- color tuning utility
    config/config.json          <- camera, slots, routes, HSV, engine config
    config/schedule.csv         <- daily driver -> route assignments
    data/faces/<driver_id>/     <- enrolled driver photos (you create)
    data/logs/key_events.db     <- SQLite event log (auto-created)
    evidence/                   <- MP4 evidence clips (auto-created)
    models/                     <- auto-downloaded model assets (ignored)
    src/
        color_detector.py       <- HSV slot analysis (PRESENT/ABSENT/...)
        camera.py               <- threaded capture + evidence buffer
        hand_tracker.py         <- MediaPipe hand-vs-slot intersection
        face_recognizer.py      <- InsightFace driver identification
        schedule_manager.py     <- schedule CSV loader
        database.py             <- SQLite checkouts manager
        alarm_system.py         <- overlays, signage, chimes/alarms
    tests/                      <- unit tests (python -m pytest tests/)

